{"ts": "2026-10-31T14:44:04.623346Z", "run_id": "r-20261031T1443Z-9f29", "unit": "openspec:add-admin-cli", "seq": 5, "type": "human.verdict", "question": "Q-002", "target": "C-a13f7327", "decision": "accept", "by": "delegate", "reason": "The criterion is satisfied by the code as it stands. The judge's doubt was that revoke-role might answer an unknown or foreign user with \"not held\" and exit 0. It cannot: revokeRole in src/identity/rbac/grants.ts runs requireUserId, requireRoleId, requireRole and requireUser before its DELETE, exactly as grantRole does, so \"Bad Role\" throws RBAC_INVALID, the role \"ghost\" and the user \"usr_ghost\" throw RBAC_NOT_FOUND, and bob of tenant_b is not found by the tenant-scoped user lookup for tenant_a and throws RBAC_NOT_FOUND too; the boolean \"absent\" branch is reached only after those checks pass. The revoke-role command is a thin wrapper over revokeRole with the same required-option handling as grant-role, and both go through the same defineAdminCommand, whose describeError marks any RBAC_* code as a refusal, prints \"CODE: message\", writes the cli.command_run event with outcome \"refused\" and that code, and exits 1. The tracing test exercises that whole path for grant-role (RBAC_NOT_FOUND, ", "text_sha256": "6e386d0e059b1b170a6aa69545e374207eeff9d4fa2a841eed2b1dafb1c58bb2", "answer_sha256": "bb2536bf7f15a20e3c2574753eecfafbde033b5bc1048dcacd388558f63d0087", "profile": "delegate-fable", "model": "claude-fable-5-1", "cli": "claude-code"}
{"ts": "2026-10-31T14:44:04.624228Z", "run_id": "r-20261031T1443Z-9f29", "unit": "openspec:add-admin-cli", "seq": 6, "type": "human.verdict", "question": "Q-003", "target": "F-f42f8c30", "decision": "accept", "by": "delegate", "reason": "The finding rests on a hypothesis the code rules out, so it may be waived; the behaviour the scenario asks of revoke-role is in place and the shared path is exercised. The judge feared that revokeRole might return false for an unknown or foreign user, which would make revoke-role print \"not held\" and exit 0. It cannot: revokeRole in src/identity/rbac/grants.ts runs requireUserId and requireRoleId (RBAC_INVALID for \"Bad Role\"), then requireRole and requireUser (RBAC_NOT_FOUND for an unknown role or a user not in the chosen tenant, since the lookup is scoped by tenant_id) before its DELETE, in the same order as grantRole; the \"absent\" branch is reached only after those checks pass, so bob of tenant_b is refused for tenant_a and his author grant is untouched. The revoke-role command is a thin wrapper that passes the arguments through and maps only the boolean, and both commands go through the same defineAdminCommand, whose describeError marks any RBAC_* code as refused, prints \"CODE: mess", "text_sha256": "a53369232592feb55c5499c26973f8d29ca31d9f9a071776e64810fb0abd0468", "answer_sha256": "c477ef00b43cade944b4aafd80700ba965654c01348c657129201f8c40d13809", "profile": "delegate-fable", "model": "claude-fable-5-1", "cli": "claude-code"}
